			<div class="content" id="post-content-4795714">
									

<p>Not a certified crit nor I can give greenlights.</p>
<p>Take my comments with a grain of salt.</p>
<p>Let's see</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;CRIT:</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The Foundation discovered this space manipulating anomaly at the edges of the Solar System while Voyager was passing through the Oort Cloud. Voyager captured an image of it believing it was a distant star and sent it to NASA’s JPL. An agent infiltrated there has confiscated the image and brought it to the SCP Foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why an agent would confiscate an image of a distant star? what is the purpose of this? Did the foundation know beforehand about this anomaly? and if so, why bothering messing with NASA, when they just could take containment mesearures beforehand.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The anomaly sends every subject that enters it to an alternate dimension. The anomaly is also in a collision course with Earth and will collide with it in the year 2060.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How?, how do we know this? Voyager didn't carry any person and that trip took more than 30 years.</p>
<p>So how can you justify the &quot;subjects eating process&quot;?</p>
<p>Again, the SCP was detected in the Oort cloud, very, very far away from our planet, how did the foundation managed to detect the nature of the object? and what is more puzzling how did it manage to see the interior of it?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All the containment procedures available to the Foundation right now are irrelevant and will not stop the object from colliding with Earth. Thus the Foundation prepared a task force to theatre the anomaly with a reality anchor to blast it out of existence.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What would a Task Force do in the first place? I mean, if they are sending reality anchors I think that reserchers are more suitable for that job.</p>
<p>What is the point of the article if you are destroying the anomaly so fast? I think you should focus on creating a more structured anomaly rather than thinking how destroying it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This wormhole is not particularly dangerous, the real danger is the entities located inside of it. Exploration of the SCP has revealed what appeared to be sentient shadows that inhabit the other side of the oddity. The shadows are almost uncontainable, their only known weakness being high levels of light.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So far you have presented an space time anomaly not something related to wormholes or singularities.</p>
<p>An space time disruption can be produce by a number of reasons, usually related with objects with great mass, but even a Kugelblitz a theoretical light based singularity can be considered an space time anomaly.</p>
<p>The point I'm making is that &quot;the wormhole&quot; thing is not something that will happen just because you are talking about an space time anomaly and remember, even now an Einstein-Rosen bridge (wormholes) are only theoretical.</p>
<p>I think you need to justify why this anomaly is creating a wormhole in the first place and how the wormhole is affecting us.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The strange thing is that the oddity began moving towards Earth only after a human expedition firstly encountered the shadows while in an exploration expedition in the year 2043. This implied that the shadows somehow tracked back the ship here, on Earth. Possibly by infiltrating the vessel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think that at this point I'm totally lost, what kind of space time anomaly are we talking about? An incredible big space ship?, a star? a blackhole?&#8230;</p>
<p>I don't understand the &quot;shadow thing&quot;</p>
<p>Maybe you can expand on the shadow figures, their meaning and what they might do.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Hope these observations help</p>

