			<div class="content" id="post-content-4780633">
									

<p>So, my issue with this as an ending is that it's far too simple and neat. They have a big weird set of satellites that blow the anomaly away, the end. It feels like a copout. There's this ticking time bomb on an anomaly that isn't going to stop coming towards Earth and then they just&#8230; find a relatively simple way to fix the problem, and do it. It feels like there's no actual challenge to the Foundation there. Sure it has to be done with high precision, but that's just a mild bit of tension in the moment, which is unlikely to come through well in the short space this event will likely be conveyed in on the page.</p>
<p>On top of that, this conclusion doesn't really feel like it pays off on anything or otherwise make use of any elements established earlier in the story. Why is it <em>this</em> method specifically that you've chosen? It feels arbitrary and unearned by the preceding event, which is also likely to make it feel like an unsatisfying ending.</p>
<p>I think more thought here needs to be put into setup and payoff here, especially with regards to the ending; additionally, the ending needs to feel like more of a challenge, where the victory by the Foundation feels earned and not arbitrary.</p>

							</div>

								
					
