<p>Okay so this is stronger, but I'm not clear on what the result of all this is. Do they succeed? To what degree do they succeed? Is the reader doing to learn whether they succeed at all?</p>
<p>I think part of the issue here for me is that there's no human element, it's difficult to become invested in such a large danger and story when there's nothing to anchor the reader to the core fear of the story and keep them grounded in a realistic depiction of this story.</p>
<p>Additionally, the ending here still feels rather&#8230; neat? I'm not sure how to describe it, but this doesn't feel like it extrapolates well on the tension present in the premise - it just extends it by adding on more things for the reader to care about and feel the tension as a result of; there's nothing to make the reader actually invested.</p>
<p>Again, I think there's potential in the premise, but this version of the story still feels overly focused on containment procedures and trying to avoid danger rather than the actual fear and terror of this anomaly, and what it might do to the people experiencing it. This is equivalent to a large asteroid hitting earth and wiping it out, and this is a version of that narrative focused on the intricate details of how the missile that blows it up is built, rather than the people doing the building.</p>
<p>It's possible what you have in mind for this article just isn't for me personally, but right now it's not clicking for me because of the impersonal nature of the story and the neatness of the ending.</p>

